WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.965
Marcus is 38, works at a logistics company and has two children.

00:00:03.965 --> 00:00:06.608
His question is simple: what happens to the

00:00:06.608 --> 00:00:09.252
family budget if someone gets sick or hurt?

00:00:09.252 --> 00:00:10.904
That question is about risk.

00:00:10.904 --> 00:00:13.547
In insurance, risk means uncertainty about a loss.

00:00:13.547 --> 00:00:16.190
Marcus does not know whether anyone in his

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:18.503
household will need a hospital this year.

00:00:18.503 --> 00:00:20.155
That uncertainty is the risk.

00:00:20.155 --> 00:00:22.468
Exposure means being subject to that possibility.

00:00:22.468 --> 00:00:25.772
Everyone with a body is exposed to illness and injury.

00:00:25.772 --> 00:00:28.085
Health insurance does not remove the exposure.

00:00:28.085 --> 00:00:31.720
It addresses the financial consequences when an uncertain event actually happens.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:34.722
A peril is the cause of a loss.

00:00:34.722 --> 00:00:38.475
In health insurance the perils are sickness and accidental injury.

00:00:38.475 --> 00:00:41.477
A hazard is a condition that increases the

00:00:41.477 --> 00:00:44.479
chance of a loss or makes it worse.

00:00:44.479 --> 00:00:47.106
Icy front steps are a physical hazard.

00:00:47.106 --> 00:00:50.858
Dishonesty, such as exaggerating a claim, is a moral hazard.

00:00:50.858 --> 00:00:53.860
Carelessness that grows because a person feels covered,

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.862
like skipping a seatbelt, is a morale hazard.

00:00:56.862 --> 00:00:59.864
When a question describes a condition that raises

00:00:59.864 --> 00:01:02.491
the odds, it is describing a hazard.

00:01:02.491 --> 00:01:05.493
When it names the event that actually caused

00:01:05.493 --> 00:01:08.120
the bills, it is naming the peril.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.313
Loss is the financial consequence.

00:01:10.313 --> 00:01:13.821
For Marcus a loss could be a hospital

00:01:13.821 --> 00:01:17.329
bill, a prescription, or wages missed while recovering.

00:01:17.329 --> 00:01:20.398
Notice that each is measured in money.

00:01:20.398 --> 00:01:26.537
Insurance deals with pure risk, where the only outcomes are loss or no loss.

00:01:26.537 --> 00:01:28.730
Getting sick offers no upside.

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:34.869
Speculative risk includes a chance of gain, like starting a business or buying shares.

00:01:34.869 --> 00:01:38.377
Insurers do not sell protection against speculative risk.

00:01:38.377 --> 00:01:43.639
On a question, first ask whether the situation could produce a gain.

00:01:43.639 --> 00:01:49.340
If it could, it is speculative and not insurable in the ordinary sense.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:50.922
Health policies define two perils.

00:01:50.922 --> 00:01:53.771
Accidental injury is bodily harm caused by an unexpected

00:01:53.771 --> 00:01:56.619
external event, such as a fall on the ice.

00:01:56.619 --> 00:02:00.417
Sickness is an illness or disease that first shows itself while the

00:02:00.417 --> 00:02:04.215
policy is in force, such as the strep throat Marcus’s daughter develops.

00:02:04.215 --> 00:02:05.481
The distinction has consequences.

00:02:05.481 --> 00:02:07.696
An accident-only policy pays nothing for sickness.

00:02:07.696 --> 00:02:12.127
Many policies apply a probationary period to sickness but not to accidents, so an

00:02:12.127 --> 00:02:16.242
illness in the first weeks may be excluded while an accident is covered.

00:02:16.242 --> 00:02:19.090
Accidental death and dismemberment benefits respond only to accidents.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:22.066
Older policies used an accidental means definition.

00:02:22.066 --> 00:02:26.318
Both the cause and the result had to be unintended.

00:02:26.318 --> 00:02:31.421
If Marcus deliberately lifted a heavy box and hurt his back, the

00:02:31.421 --> 00:02:36.523
lifting was intentional, so a strict means policy could deny the claim.

00:02:36.523 --> 00:02:41.200
Modern policies use an accidental results, or accidental bodily injury, definition.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:47.153
The injury need only be unexpected and unintended, even if the activity was deliberate.

00:02:47.153 --> 00:02:51.830
The results definition is broader and more favorable to the insured,

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:56.082
and many states, including Maryland, restrict the narrower means wording.

00:02:56.082 --> 00:03:01.610
On the exam, read which definition the policy uses before deciding the claim.

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:02.669
Return to Marcus.

00:03:02.669 --> 00:03:06.906
The possibility that someone in his home becomes ill is the risk.

00:03:06.906 --> 00:03:10.437
His family’s membership in the human race is the exposure.

00:03:10.437 --> 00:03:12.202
Strep throat is a peril.

00:03:12.202 --> 00:03:15.027
The icy steps outside are a physical hazard.

00:03:15.027 --> 00:03:17.498
The emergency room bill is the loss.

00:03:17.498 --> 00:03:22.441
If his policy defines accidents by results, a strained back from lifting is covered.

00:03:22.441 --> 00:03:25.972
If it only covers accidents, his daughter’s illness is not.

00:03:25.972 --> 00:03:29.856
Every health question starts by sorting the facts into these words.

00:03:29.856 --> 00:03:33.739
Do that first, and the rest of the course becomes a

00:03:33.739 --> 00:03:37.270
matter of applying rules to a situation you already understand.
